{use crate::view::layouts::{Shell, Width}}
{use crate::view::ui::{Action, Panel}}
<Shell chrome={self.chrome.clone()} width={Width::Wide}>

  <section class="pt-16 pb-4 text-center sm:pt-24">
    <p class="font-mono text-[0.75rem] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-madder">{self.content.eyebrow}</p>

    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-[19ch] font-display text-[clamp(2.6rem,6.2vw,4.4rem)] font-normal
               leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.032em] text-ink">{self.content.title}</h1>

    <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-[58ch] font-serif text-[clamp(1.06rem,1.7vw,1.25rem)] leading-[1.62] text-ink-soft">
      {@html self.content.lede_html}
    </p>

    <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      {#for action in &self.content.actions}
        <Action href={action.href.clone()} primary={action.primary} class="">{action.label}</Action>
      {/for}
    </div>

    {# The dependency line, in the first screen. A reader who is convinced by the
       hero should be able to start from it rather than go looking.

       The block is centred; the code inside it is not. Centred code is unreadable
       — the eye needs a common left edge to return to. #}
    <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-[24rem]">
      <div class="text-left">{@html self.install}</div>
      <p class="mt-2.5 font-ui text-[0.78rem] text-ink-soft">{self.content.install.note}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#
    The weave. The component on the left — the struct and the template that share
    its name — and on the right the call that uses it above the markup it
    produced. The grid is the argument: the site's whole claim is that those
    files *are* that last panel, produced at build time rather than at request
    time.

    The call earns its place by making the output panel readable. The card's body
    comes from the caller and its footer from a slot's fallback, so with no call
    on the page both look like things the template invented — the two `<slot>`s
    in `card.dmk` pay off only once there is something filling one of them and
    something leaving the other alone.

    Which is also why "compiles to" sits between the call and the output rather
    than between the columns: that is the pair it is true of. Between the columns
    the relation is *declares* and *uses*, which the filenames already say.

    The two columns hold different amounts of code, so the right one would end
    short and leave the block looking unfinished. Both are stretched to the
    taller, and the right one gives its slack to the two panels rather than to
    the gaps: `grow` on each lets them keep their natural size as a floor and
    share whatever is left over, so all four panels are of a size and the
    divider stays the fixed thing between them.

    The extra height is not dead space — `.code-scroll` carries the panel's own
    surface and its edge shadows, so a panel that grew simply has more of the
    same page under its last line.

    It stacks below `lg` rather than scrolling sideways — two columns of code on
    a phone is two columns of nothing legible — and the stacked order still reads
    declare, declare, call, output.
  #}
  <section class="mt-16 sm:mt-20" aria-label="What a component is">
    <div class="grid items-stretch gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-4">
        <Panel name={self.content.weave.rs_name.clone()} code={self.rs.clone()} derived={false} class=""/>
        <Panel name={self.content.weave.dmk_name.clone()} code={self.dmk.clone()} derived={false} class=""/>
      </div>

      <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-4">
        <Panel name={self.content.weave.call_name.clone()} code={self.call.clone()} derived={false} class="grow"/>

        <div class="flex items-center justify-center">
          <span class="h-px w-10 bg-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="mx-3 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-rule bg-surface px-3 py-1
                       font-mono text-[0.68rem] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-ink-faint">
            compiles to
          </span>
          <span class="h-px w-10 bg-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>

        <Panel name={self.content.weave.out_name.clone()} code={self.out.clone()} derived={true} class="grow"/>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#
    The features, set as a specimen sheet rather than as a grid of cards.

    Three devices, all borrowed from print and all already in this page's
    vocabulary. A hanging mono numeral gives the section a reading order and
    makes the count look chosen rather than however-many-there-are. A hairline
    above each entry turns the grid into a ledger — which is also what makes an
    odd number of features look finished instead of orphaned, since a last row
    of one reads as the end of a table rather than as a gap. And the section
    finally says its own name, in the mono eyebrow the hero already uses,
    dropped to faint ink so it announces the section without competing with the
    title above it.

    No entry hover: none of this is interactive, and a card that lifts under the
    cursor would be promising a link that isn't there.
  #}
  <section class="mt-24 sm:mt-32" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why" class="font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-ink-faint">Why Damask</h2>

    <div class="mt-7 grid gap-x-10 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {#for (n, feature) in self.features.iter().enumerate()}
        {# `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than `1fr`: a grid track's implicit minimum is
           its content, so a code panel — which is wider than a phone and scrolls
           inside itself — would otherwise widen the column and take the whole
           document sideways with it. #}
        <article class="grid min-w-0 grid-cols-[2.25rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-y-2.5
                        border-t border-rule pt-6 pb-11">
          <span class="font-mono text-[0.7rem] tabular-nums tracking-[0.08em] text-madder"
                aria-hidden="true">{format!("{:02}", n + 1)}</span>

          <h3 class="font-display text-[1.35rem] font-medium tracking-[-0.015em] text-ink">{feature.title}</h3>

          <p class="col-start-2 font-serif text-[1rem] leading-[1.66] text-ink-soft">{@html feature.body_html}</p>

          {#if let Some(code) = &feature.code}
            <div class="col-start-2 mt-1.5">{@html code}</div>
          {/if}
        </article>
      {/for}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-24 sm:mt-32">
    <h2 class="font-display text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.4vw,2.5rem)] font-normal tracking-[-0.025em] text-ink">
      {self.content.closing_title}
    </h2>
    <div class="prose mt-6 max-w-[68ch]">{@html self.content.closing}</div>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Action href={self.book_href.clone()} primary={true} class="">Read the book</Action>
      <Action href={self.docs_href.clone()} primary={false} class="">Browse the reference</Action>
    </div>
  </section>
</Shell>
